  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure remains large-and-in-charge this week as the Omega \nBlock situated across the U.S. continues to collapse. The center of \nthe subtropical ridge, objectively analyzed at 591 dam per the 12Z \nUA charts, was centered over the Deep South, with its apex eclipsing \nthe CWA. Farther north, a shortwave trough was digging into far \neastern Canada; and the synoptic cold front associated with this \nsystem will weaken as it moves into the region Tuesday. Westerly \nwinds today will transition towards the northeast post-FROPA, with \nthe front expected to stall near or along the I-64 corridor. By the \nmiddle of the week, the Omega Block will have completely collapsed, \nwhich will allow an attenuating shortwave trough to eject over the \nOH River Valley and into the eastern Great Lakes region.\n\nAs this shortwave trough approaches to the west of the CWA, the \nbackdoor cold front that stalled across the region the previous day \nwill undergo warm-frontogenesis and lift northeastward beneath a \nthickening cirroform overcast. The net result of this should result \nin some slight improvements to moisture within the boundary-layer \ndue to the restrictions of full insolation and the presence of the \nmodified return flow. Full sunshine is set to return Thursday, as \nthe previous shortwave trough will have quickly become absorbed into \na large-scale, cyclonic gyre-like pattern over the northern \nlatitudes of North America. High temperatures will then warm into \nthe middle-upper 70s by the end of the work week as geopotential \nheight tendencies remain slightly positive from the subtropical \nridging to the south of the region.\n\nBroadly cyclonic flow is forecast to encompass the CWA this weekend, \nwith slightly cooler, but still mild, temperatures and a thickening \novercast for Halloween. Rain chances are appearing to arrive late \nSaturday night and into Sunday, but it is too soon to iron out the \nexact timing of PoPs. By the time Halloween arrives, this will be \nthe longest stretch of dry weather observed for the Charleston area \nsince the Fall of 2017, when there was no precipitation for 23 days \nin a row, with this stretch being 14 days come Halloween.",
